(524265, 51512, 5158, '<td valign="top">I was surprised not to see more skating images in this challenge. This one is the best of this genre for me. I like the composition with the tide cropping and the cold colors. Give an icy feeling. I would have set the focal plane to the tip rather then to the middle of the skate. <br/>Anyhow, good luck to you</td>', 'I was surprised not to see more skating images in this challenge. This one is the best of this genre for me. I like the composition with the tide cropping and the cold colors. Give an icy feeling. I would have set the focal plane to the tip rather then to the middle of the skate. Anyhow, good luck to you', '2003-12-28 04:46:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(524266, 51517, 5158, '<td valign="top">The mood of this image is great. Also the wide open mouth. I would have preferred if you have used a stone rather then wood (probably a table!?) as edge too hang on: the texture of the wood does not make it look realy. Also the graininess is a little overdone IMO.<br/>What I like is that you used B&amp;W, it works well on this image. Good luck.</td>', 'The mood of this image is great. Also the wide open mouth. I would have preferred if you have used a stone rather then wood (probably a table!?) as edge too hang on: the texture of the wood does not make it look realy. Also the graininess is a little overdone IMO.What I like is that you used B&W, it works well on this image. Good luck.', '2003-12-28 04:50:12.000000', NULL, 1),
(524277, 52107, 6388, '<td valign="top">snow is really hard to expose...it confuses the cams for the most part - overexposes snow, and underexposes everything else.  Fill flash is a great way to avoid it, but certainly not fool proof.  best bet is to expose the snow, and adjust the rest later in PS or something.  if it were a MEMBER''S challenge you could do it ;)<br/> <br/>good luck,<br/> <br/>Pedro</td>', 'snow is really hard to expose...it confuses the cams for the most part - overexposes snow, and underexposes everything else.  Fill flash is a great way to avoid it, but certainly not fool proof.  best bet is to expose the snow, and adjust the rest later in PS or something.  if it were a MEMBER''S challenge you could do it ;) good luck, Pedro', '2003-12-28 05:06:40.000000', NULL, 1),
(524282, 51375, 6388, '<td valign="top">nice.  great colour, and i love the diagonal line this makes.i''d have preferred you crop out the foreground i think, but only because the rough lines of the rock disrupt the beautifully smooth lines of the pier for me.  either way, i like it.<br/> <br/></td>', 'nice.  great colour, and i love the diagonal line this makes.i''d have preferred you crop out the foreground i think, but only because the rough lines of the rock disrupt the beautifully smooth lines of the pier for me.  either way, i like it. ', '2003-12-28 05:17:49.000000', NULL, 1),
(524284, 51872, 6388, '<td valign="top">two little points that I''ll make, and only because this was a ''studio'' type shot that gives you the freedom to setit up ''just so...''.<br/> <br/>anyway, the thermometer is a little bit rotated (maybe just the way it had to be), and cropping off the bottom of it throws the balance off a little.  I''d have left a little foreground i think.<br/> <br/>anyway - nice colour, and good idea.<br/> <br/>Pedro</td>', 'two little points that I''ll make, and only because this was a ''studio'' type shot that gives you the freedom to setit up ''just so...''. anyway, the thermometer is a little bit rotated (maybe just the way it had to be), and cropping off the bottom of it throws the balance off a little.  I''d have left a little foreground i think. anyway - nice colour, and good idea. Pedro', '2003-12-28 05:23:01.000000', NULL, 1),
